## Why This Is Asked
This question probes real-world guardrails for implementing Azure OpenAI in multi-tenant applications, focusing on prompt governance, data redaction, role-based access control, and auditable data flows.

## Key Concepts
- Guardrails microservice deployed behind Azure API Management
- Versioned policy knowledge base stored in Azure Blob Storage with Cognitive Search for retrieval
- Per-tenant RBAC enforcement through Azure Active Directory with strict data-scope isolation
- PHI/PII redaction for both inputs and outputs using regex patterns and named entity recognition
- Comprehensive audit logging to Azure Data Lake for compliance and forensic analysis
- Test coverage for redaction accuracy, policy drift detection, and RBAC enforcement validation

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactInput(input, piiRegexes) {
  let output = input;
  for (const regex of piiRegexes) {
    output = output.replace(regex, '[REDACTED]');
  }
  return output;
}

async function validatePrompt(prompt, policyKB, tenantId) {
  const embeddings = await generateEmbeddings(prompt);
  const relevantPolicies = await searchKnowledgeBase(policyKB, embeddings);
  return checkCompliance(prompt, relevantPolicies);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design data minimization, policy grounding, and auditable RBAC in a multi-tenant environment using Azure OpenAI; it tests integration points and test strategy.

## Key Concepts
- guided prompt design
- per-tenant RBAC with Azure AD
- Cognitive Search KB grounding
- immutable Data Lake logs
- deterministic runbooks

## Code Example
```javascript
async function boundaryDecision(user, request) {
  const ctx = await getTenantContext(user.tenantId); // from Azure AD
  const policy = await queryKB("policy", ctx);
  const subset = applyMinimization(request.data, policy, ctx);
  // force RBAC at data layer
  if (!hasAccess(user, subset)) throw new Error("Access denied");
  const rationale = `Selected ${subset.length} records for tenant ${ctx.name} per policy.`;
  await logAudit({user, request, subset, ctx});
  return { subset, rationale };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data minimization guarantees end-to-end? 
- How do you handle policy KB drift and versioning? 
- What latency budgets and retries would you enforce for OpenAI calls?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to architect a governance-heavy, production-ready OpenAI integration across tenants, including data redaction, KB grounding, per-tenant state, and actionable remediation.

## Key Concepts
- Azure OpenAI moderation and content safety
- Text Analytics for redaction
- Cognitive Search KB grounding with versioning
- Cosmos DB per-tenant state
- Azure Boards for remediation/workflow
- Canary deployments and end-to-end auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-pipeline orchestration sketch
async function evaluate(tp) {
  const moderated = await mod.check(tp.prompt, tp.response);
  const redacted = redactor.redact(moderated);
  const grounded = kb.ground(redacted, tp.tenantId);
  const risk = riskStore.update(tp.tenantId, grounded.score);
  board.createTask(tp.tenantId, risk, grounded.details);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement tenant isolation and key management to prevent data leaks across tenants?
- What metrics and alerting would you introduce to detect degradation or drift in evaluation quality?

